{"qid":"Q1136188","names":["Saadi Sultanate"],"title":"Saadi Sultanate","endonym":{"native":"سعديون","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"The Saadi (Sa'dian) Sultanate was a Moroccan dynasty of sharifi descent that emerged from the Draa valley c.1510 and expelled the Portuguese from Agadir in 1541, reunifying Morocco against both Portuguese coastal enclaves and Ottoman pressure from Algiers. Its apogee came under Ahmad al-Mansur (r.1578–1603), who crushed a Portuguese invasion at the Battle of the Three Kings (Wadi al-Makhazin, 1578) and in 1591 dispatched an army under Judar Pasha across the Sahara to shatter the Songhai Empire at the Battle of Tondibi, temporarily extending Saadi influence to Timbuktu and the Niger Bend. After al-Mansur's death the dynasty fragmented into warring branches; the last Saadi sultan was killed in 1659 by the rising Alaoui dynasty.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core Moroccan territory is reasonably attested from Portuguese and Ottoman sources and internal chronicles; the trans-Saharan extent of Saadi influence after 1591 is approximated based on chronicle accounts and has significant spatial uncertainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Saadi Sultanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saadi_Sultanate"},{"label":"Britannica: Marinid dynasty (Morocco context)","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Marinid-dynasty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Saadi Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Muhammad al-Qaim bi-Amr Allah","nativeName":"محمد القائم بأمر الله","roman":"Muḥammad al-Qāʾim bi-Amr Allāh","start":1509,"end":1517,"image":null,"imageUrl":nul